Passing parameters to functions:
when a function calls another function the caller function has to allocate shadow space that is suffcient to hold 4 parameters values for the callee even though the callee does not need that much, this shadow space is used to store function arguments that are passed in to the callee in registers. any parameters beyond the four parameters have to be stored in the stack before the shadow store. this is called fast-call calling convention.

NOTE: so whenever you see a stack allocation that is too much for a function call so you should understand that this space is for the shadow store.

NOTE: so as discussed above the caller has to allocate a shadow space that is suffcient to save 4 parameter values so that the callee can either decide to save the parameters there or not.

Calling Conventions:
NOTE: calling conventions are compiler dependent.
1.registers: the caller should assume that the callee will change the valotile registers and should save them somewhere before calling the callee.
	the following registers are the voliatile registers:
	-Visual Studio: RAX, RCX, RDX, R8, R9, R10, R11
	-GCC: RAX, RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8, R9, R10, R11
	These are called "Caller-save registers"

	Caller-save registers:
	are registers that the caller should assume that they might be changed by the callee.
	they belong to the callee.

	Callee-save registers:
	are registers that should not be changed by the callee
	they belong to the caller.
	Visual Studio: RBX, RBP, RDI, RSI, R12-R15
	GCC: RBX, RBP, R12-R15

2.parameters passing:
	x86-64 compilers use the caller-saved registers to pass parameters and return values as well.
	RAX: is going to be used to return back values. or RDX:RAX can be used to return 128bit value.

	in Microsoft "x64" ABI:
	parameters are passed respectivly in RCX, RDX, R8, R9
	remaning parameters are pushed into the stack.

	in System V "x86-x64" ABI:
	parameters are passed respectivly in RDI, RSI, RDX, RCX, R8, R9
	remaning parameters are pushed into the stack.


	32-bit calling conventions:
	1. cdecl   : caller must clean up the stack (most c code)
	2. stdcall : callee must clean up the stack (win32 api code)

NOTE: 1 common thing in stdcall and cdecl 32-bit calling conventions is the functions prolog:
